    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17782",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/efc577f386353b4120dec733e5e6a627ad2412744dce4d71deba2b500a1edec2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}x-1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/2891d009bd6f5d026ca47afe90f3ec4d6fafc654b7212392b325f76fe43f806e.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/ab1f210741e01139618dc2fa1347cf1591cb90ca4f67906b89ebc7df29d77f32.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/e5552a14dc62407e02bb8f5e633ae194a95d313bce04abb3186b56ce83ec2974.png)",
